\subsection{32B Scale}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

Results at 32B are suggestive due to small test sets ($n{=}19$--$20$; MDE 8.6--9.5pp at 80\% power). R1-32B shows early detection ($W{=}1$, +14.2pp over position) but the residualized gap (+1.9pp) is not significant; QwQ-32B achieves significance at all window sizes but with negative lead time and a competitive position baseline. Neither provides confirmatory evidence; replication with $n \geq 50$ traces is needed (\cref{app:32b_full}).\looseness=-1

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities (\cref{fig:window_ablation}; cross-model and text-probe ablations in \cref{tab:window_full,app:text_window}): at 7--8B, the modality advantage (+20.1 to +21.9pp) exceeds temporal improvement (+13pp). GRU models achieve comparable accuracy but higher FPR (\cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:temporal_details}); an adaptive two-stage strategy offers marginal gains (\cref{app:two_stage}). CP-aligned probe trajectories confirm sharper HS transitions at the commitment point (\cref{fig:trajectory}). An oracle analysis (\cref{sec:oracle}) places the early detection ceiling at 50--94\% across models, framing the gap that learned probes must close.\looseness=-1

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

